module Core

import Collections
import Numerics.Integer
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shortest digits by Schubfach (B16 / B32 / B64)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Giulietti's Schubfach ("The Schubfach way to render doubles", 2020; the algorithm behind Java's
# Double.toString since JDK 19) finds the same shortest decimal as the exact interval method above with
# fixed-width integers: the value c * 2^q and its rounding interval are scaled by a 126-bit approximation
# of 10^-k (k chosen from q, so the interval spans less than one unit at the next decimal position up),
# each product rounded to odd so every comparison with a candidate stays exact. Of the candidates of the
# length one below and the length at that scale, it keeps the one inside the interval, and between two it
# keeps the one closer to the value (the even one on a tie). The table serves every binary format up to
# B64. Java always prints two digits, so it skips the shorter candidates below s = 100 and rescales the
# tiniest subnormals; here the shorter candidates are tried from s = 10 on, which gives the plain shortest
# decimal everywhere, as before.

### g(k) = floor(10^-k * 2^(125 - floor(log2(10^-k)))) + 1 for k = -324 .. 292, each as g1 * 2^63 + g0
### (entry 2(k + 324) is g1, the next g0).
